-q
----------------------------------------------------------------------
10 CLS
20 B% = 5
30 C% = -5
40 F = 2.5
' CSNG converts the value to Single, see 5.2.2. A constant argument is
' folded by the compiler, a variable goes through ITOF.
50 A% = CSNG(5) = 5.0
55 PRINT "-1", A%
60 A% = CSNG(B%) = 5.0
65 PRINT "-1", A%
70 A% = CSNG(-5) = -5.0
75 PRINT "-1", A%
80 A% = CSNG(C%) = -5.0
85 PRINT "-1", A%
' The result really is a Single: the divisions below keep the fraction
90 A% = CSNG(B%) / 2 = 2.5
95 PRINT "-1", A%
100 A% = CSNG(B%) / 4 = 1.25
105 PRINT "-1", A%
' An argument that is already Single keeps its value, and the compiler
' warns that the call has no effect
110 A% = CSNG(F) = 2.5
115 PRINT "-1", A%
120 G = CSNG(F)
130 A% = G = 2.5
135 PRINT "-1", A%
' A constant Single argument is folded, so there is no warning for it
140 A% = CSNG(2.5) = 2.5
145 PRINT "-1", A%
' An Integer expression as the argument; the constants need the % sign,
' without it they are Single and the whole expression becomes Single too
150 A% = CSNG(B% + 1%) = 6.0
155 PRINT "-1", A%
160 A% = CSNG(B% * 2%) / 4 = 2.5
165 PRINT "-1", A%
' A Single expression as the argument warns, same as a Single variable
170 A% = CSNG(F + 1) = 3.5
175 PRINT "-1", A%
----------------------------------------------------------------------
WARNING: at 22:10 line 110 - CSNG function call has no effect, value already has Single type.
WARNING: at 24:9 line 120 - CSNG function call has no effect, value already has Single type.
WARNING: at 37:10 line 170 - CSNG function call has no effect, value already has Single type.
